  "query_preview": "(Today through Friday)...\nIssued at 135 AM EDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nToday Through Sunday.\n\nSurface high pressure migrating exiting the Mid-Atlantic coast will \nmaintain dry conditions and southeasterly flow across central \nIndiana on through the day today with increasing cloud cover. These \nclouds will keep temperatures cooler across the southeastern \ncounties with highs in the mid 70s while areas towards Lafayette may \nget close to 80 as the thicker clouds don't arrive until mid to late \nafternoon.\n\nPrimary focus remains in the overnight through Sunday timeframe as \nthe remnants of Isaias move into the Ohio Valley. There remains \nconsiderable spread among deterministic and ensemble guidance \nregarding the western extent of the associated precipitation shield. \nThe latest GFS and GEFS solutions continue to favor an eastward \ntrack, keeping the primary moisture axis and associated heavier rain \nfocused over Ohio and eastern Kentucky. In this scenario, rainfall \namounts will be fairly minimal with the potential that rain amounts \nremain a quarter inch or less for much of the areas west of I-69.\n\nConversely, the NAM and a few higher resolution models support a \nslightly deeper phasing of the northern and southern streams, \npulling the remnant low and associated mid-level deformation zone \nfurther west. This solution brings a broad shield of light to \nmoderate rain as far west as the Illinois border, yielding 0.25 to \n0.75 inches of rainfall for the western half of the CWA and amounts \ngreater than an inch towards the southeast. High-resolution guidance \nfrom the HREF highlights a remarkably sharp precipitation gradient \non the western periphery of the system. Strong easterly flow aloft \nwill encounter a persistent dry airmass over the mid-Mississippi \nValley which leans more towards the lower-end precipitation \nsolution, especially for the western and northwestern portions of \nthe forecast area.\n\nRegarding timing, isentropic ascent and saturation will gradually \novercome the dry antecedent airmass after 21Z Saturday, with peak \nprecipitation rates occurring between 00Z and 06Z. Forcing will \nquickly exit to the east by Sunday morning as the primary trough \naxis pivots eastward with any precipitation remaining closer to the \nOhio border. High temperatures will range from the upper 70s in the \nwest where clouds will clear early to around 70 in the east where \nclouds will stick around through the day.\n\nMonday Through Friday.\n\nMid-level ridging and surface high pressure will build across the \nregion on Monday, initiating a period of stronger WAA and bringing \nnear record temperatures to Illinois and portions of western \nIndiana. These much warmer than normal temperatures will continue \ninto Tuesday before the pattern begins to shift towards mid to late \nweek.\n\nMedium-range guidance show a more significant pattern amplification \nfor the middle and end of next week. A deep longwave trough will \neject out of the Rockies on Wednesday, driving rapid cyclogenesis \nover the Upper Midwest. Strong CVA and favorable upper-level jet \ncoupling will provide robust dynamic forcing across the Ohio Valley. \nA sweeping cold front will cross central Indiana late Wednesday into \nWednesday night, accompanied by widespread precipitation. \n\nPost-frontal CAA will bring temperatures back to near normal by \nFriday with high pressure building across the Ohio Valley. Looking \ntowards the weekend, this cooler pattern along with a drier airmass \nmay bring the first potential for frost Saturday night into Sunday.",
